AH = "This lesson summarises ideas from Atomic Habits by James Clear in our own words."
MIY = "This lesson summarises ideas from The Mountain Is You by Brianna Wiest in our own words."
from b2 import L as _L2
_L2['ah-four-laws']['sections'][4] = (_L2['ah-four-laws']['sections'][4][0], _L2['ah-four-laws']['sections'][4][1] + " You do not need to use all four laws at once. Often, improving just the weakest one is enough to tip a habit from 'sometimes' to 'usually'.")
_L2['ah-environment']['sections'][4] = (_L2['ah-environment']['sections'][4][0], _L2['ah-environment']['sections'][4][1] + " A useful exercise is to walk through your home and ask, for each room, which habits it currently makes easy, and whether those are the habits you actually want.")
L = {}
L['ah-invisible'] = dict(
intro="Building good habits gets most of the attention, but breaking unwanted ones matters just as much. Atomic Habits offers a mirror image of its first law: if making a cue obvious helps a habit start, then making a cue invisible helps a habit fade. This lesson explores how to reduce exposure to temptation instead of fighting it. " + AH,
sections=[
("Why resisting temptation is tiring", "Many of us try to break habits by resisting: the biscuits are on the table, and we tell ourselves not to eat them. The phone is beside us, and we try not to pick it up. The book suggests that relying on self-control in tempting situations is hard to sustain. Each moment of resistance takes effort, and over a long day that effort runs low. People who seem disciplined, the book argues, are often simply spending less time in tempting situations."),
("Reduce exposure", "The practical idea is to remove or hide the cues that trigger unwanted habits. If you watch too much TV, unplug it after each use or move it out of the bedroom. If you snack late at night, do not keep snacks in sight. If you check your phone constantly, leave it in another room while you work. The aim is not to rely on willpower in each moment, but to arrange things so the temptation rarely appears."),
("Cues are everywhere online", "Digital life is full of cues designed to draw attention: notifications, badges, autoplay, infinite feeds. Turning off non-essential notifications, moving distracting apps off your home screen, or logging out of sites after use are all ways of making cues less visible. These small frictions can create a pause in which you choose deliberately instead of reacting automatically."),
("Habits can be remembered, not erased", "The book notes that once a habit has formed, its pattern can stay in memory even after you stop. That is why old habits can return when you are back in the old environment or under stress. This is not failure. It simply means it helps to keep the environment supportive even after a habit seems gone, and to be kind to yourself if an old pattern resurfaces."),
("Replacing, not only removing", "Removing a cue leaves a gap. Many unwanted habits meet a real need, such as rest, comfort or connection. It can help to place a better option where the old cue used to be: a book where the phone used to sit, a fruit bowl where the biscuit tin was. That way the need is still met, just in a way you prefer."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the phone in the drawer", "Sneha, a graphic designer in Kochi, found that she checked her phone every few minutes while working, breaking her focus. She tried to resist and kept failing. Then she tried something different: during her two main work blocks each day, she put her phone in a desk drawer, on silent, out of sight. She did not have to resist it because she could not see it. After a week, she noticed she was finishing designs faster and feeling less scattered. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Resisting temptation moment by moment is tiring and hard to sustain.","Reduce exposure by removing or hiding cues for unwanted habits.","Digital cues can be reduced with notification and home-screen changes.","Old habits can resurface; keep the environment supportive and be kind to yourself.","Replace a removed cue with a better option that meets the same need."],
reflect=["Which unwanted habit is triggered by something you can see every day?","What need might that habit be meeting, and what could meet it instead?"],
)
L['ah-one-space'] = dict(
intro="Our brains link places with activities. A bed is for sleep, a kitchen for cooking, a desk for work. But modern homes, especially small ones, often blur these lines: we work in bed, eat at our desks and scroll everywhere. Atomic Habits suggests that giving spaces clear purposes can make habits easier. This lesson explores that idea. " + AH,
sections=[
("Context becomes a cue", "The book describes how the context around us, the room, the time, the people present, can act as a cue for behaviour. Over time, a place becomes associated with the things we do there. If you always scroll your phone in bed, getting into bed starts to cue scrolling. If you always study at one table, sitting there starts to cue focus. These associations form quietly through repetition."),
("One space, one use", "A suggestion from the book is to give each space, where possible, one main use. Work at the desk, relax on the sofa, sleep in bed. When a space is mixed, its cues become mixed too, and it is harder for your mind to settle into any one mode. Keeping uses separate helps each space prompt the right behaviour."),
("When space is limited", "Many people in India live in shared or compact homes where one room may serve as bedroom, office and living room. The idea still works on a smaller scale. A particular chair can mean reading. A particular corner of the bed can mean rest, while the other side of the room means work. Even small signals help, such as a lamp you switch on only when studying, or a cloth you lay on the table when it becomes a workspace. You are creating cues, not needing more rooms."),
("Moving to change habits", "The book also notes that a new environment can make it easier to build new habits, because the old cues are absent. A holiday, a new job or a new home can be a good moment to start something fresh. You can create a mini version of this by rearranging furniture, changing where you sit, or working in a different spot for a new habit."),
("Protecting rest", "One of the most useful applications is protecting spaces for rest. If your bed is linked with work emails and late-night videos, it may become harder to wind down there. Keeping the bed, as far as possible, for sleep and calm activities helps it signal rest."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the shared-room switch", "Imran, a law student in Aligarh, shared a small room with his cousin and did everything on his bed: studying, eating, watching videos and sleeping. He found it hard to focus and hard to sleep. With little space, he made one change: he set up a folding table against the wall and committed to studying only there, with a small reading lamp he used for nothing else. The bed became for rest. Over time, sitting at the table began to feel like the signal to focus. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Places become linked with the activities we do in them.","Give spaces one main use where possible.","In small homes, use corners, chairs, lamps or cloths as cues.","A new context can make a new habit easier to start.","Protect your bed as a place for rest."],
reflect=["Which space in your home is used for too many things?","What small signal could mark a space as 'for focus' or 'for rest'?"],
)
L['ah-commitment'] = dict(
intro="Some of the most effective decisions are made once, in a calm moment, so you do not have to keep making them in tempting ones. Atomic Habits describes commitment devices: choices you make now that lock in better behaviour later. This lesson explains the idea with everyday examples. It is general learning only, not financial advice. " + AH,
sections=[
("Deciding in advance", "Our future selves often face choices when tired, rushed or tempted. A commitment device is a decision made ahead of time that shapes those future choices. The book draws on an ancient story of a sailor who asked to be tied to the mast so he could hear a dangerous song without steering towards it. The modern versions are more ordinary, but the principle is the same: use a clear-headed moment to protect yourself in a less clear-headed one."),
("Everyday examples", "Commitment devices can be simple. Buying food in single portions rather than family packs. Setting a timer that turns off the Wi-Fi router at night. Leaving your credit card at home when you go window shopping. Scheduling a workout with a friend so cancelling means letting them down. Each one makes the desired behaviour the default and the undesired behaviour more effortful."),
("Automating good choices", "The book also discusses one-time actions that keep paying off, sometimes called automation. Unsubscribing from tempting marketing emails, setting up an automatic transfer to savings on payday, or buying a better mattress once are examples of a single decision that supports good behaviour for a long time. Automation removes the need to decide again and again. Any money-related automation should suit your own situation, and for financial decisions a qualified professional is the right person to consult."),
("Making bad habits harder", "Commitment devices are especially useful for breaking habits. If you want to watch less late-night TV, a plug timer that cuts power at a set hour adds friction. If you want to spend less on impulse, deleting saved card details from shopping apps means each purchase needs a few more steps. The point is not to make things impossible, just inconvenient enough to give you a moment to reconsider."),
("Choosing with kindness", "Commitment devices work best when they are chosen by you, for your own reasons, not imposed out of shame. They are tools for supporting the person you want to be, not punishments. If a device feels too strict, loosen it and try a gentler version."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the payday transfer", "Pooja, a nurse in Nagpur, always meant to save but found that her salary seemed to disappear by month end. She decided, once, to set a standing instruction with her bank to move a small, comfortable amount into a separate account the day after salary arrived. She no longer had to decide each month. Over time, the habit ran in the background. This is an illustrative story and not financial advice; the right approach depends on each person's situation."),
takeaways=["A commitment device is a decision made in advance to shape later choices.","Make good behaviour the default and bad behaviour more effortful.","One-time actions and automation can keep paying off.","Choose commitment devices out of self-support, not shame. Not financial advice."],
reflect=["Which recurring decision tires you out? Could you decide it once instead?","What small friction would help you pause before an unwanted habit?"],
)
L['ah-contract'] = dict(
intro="It is easy to break a promise you made only to yourself. It is harder to break one someone else knows about. Atomic Habits discusses accountability and habit contracts as ways of making the cost of an unwanted habit more immediate. This lesson explores the idea gently, with a focus on supportive accountability rather than pressure. It is general learning only, not financial advice. " + AH,
sections=[
("Why private promises slip", "When you skip a habit and nobody knows, the cost often feels small and far away. The book points out that we are more likely to repeat behaviours with immediate rewards and avoid those with immediate costs. Bad habits often feel good now and cost later; good habits often cost now and pay later. Accountability brings some of that later cost into the present, so skipping feels less free."),
("Accountability partners", "One simple approach is to share your intention with someone who cares about you. A friend who knows you plan to walk each morning might ask how it went. A study partner might expect you at the library. Knowing that someone is watching, kindly, can tip the balance on a hard day. The best partners are encouraging rather than judgemental, and the arrangement works best when it is mutual."),
("Habit contracts", "The book describes a habit contract: a written agreement in which you state a habit you commit to and a consequence if you do not follow through, signed by you and one or two others. The consequence is usually small and agreed in advance. For example, someone might agree to do the household's least favourite chore if they skip their evening study. The contract makes the commitment visible and the cost of breaking it real."),
("Keeping it healthy", "Accountability can slide into pressure, guilt or conflict if it is not handled with care. It helps to choose consequences that are light-hearted rather than harsh, to keep the focus on support, and to renegotiate if life changes. Money-based penalties can create stress; if you use any, keep them small and within your means. This is not financial advice."),
("Tracking together", "Sharing a simple tracker, such as a shared note or a calendar where you both tick off habits, can make accountability feel like teamwork. Seeing someone else's ticks can be motivating, and celebrating each other's streaks makes the process more enjoyable."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the evening walk pact", "Two colleagues in Indore, Ritu and Sameer, both wanted to walk more after work but kept making excuses. They wrote a short, playful agreement: walk thirty minutes at least four evenings a week, and whoever missed more in a week would buy the other's chai on Monday. They shared a simple checklist on their phones. The stakes were tiny, but the shared commitment made it much harder to skip. This is an illustrative story and not financial advice."),
takeaways=["Private promises are easy to break because the cost feels distant.","Accountability brings the cost of skipping into the present.","A habit contract states a commitment and a small agreed consequence.","Keep accountability kind, light and mutual. Not financial advice."],
reflect=["Who in your life would be a supportive accountability partner?","What small, light-hearted consequence would actually motivate you?"],
)
L['miy-sabotage'] = dict(
intro="Self-sabotage is a familiar frustration: we want something, and then we act in ways that push it away. The Mountain Is You offers a striking reframe: self-sabotage is often not irrational at all. It may be a way of meeting a need we have not fully recognised. This lesson explores that idea with care. " + MIY,
sections=[
("What self-sabotage looks like", "Self-sabotage can take many forms: procrastinating on an important task, picking fights in a relationship that is going well, overspending right after deciding to save, or staying up late before a big day. From the outside, these look like simple failures of discipline. The book invites us to look more closely at what these behaviours might be doing for us."),
("A hidden need", "The book's central suggestion is that self-sabotaging behaviour often meets an unconscious need. Procrastination might protect us from the fear of being judged. Avoiding a promotion might protect us from the discomfort of more visibility. Clinging to a familiar but unhappy situation might protect us from the uncertainty of change. The behaviour is not random; it is a kind of protection that once made sense, even if it no longer serves us."),
("Comfort with the familiar", "The book also discusses how people tend to feel more comfortable with what is familiar, even if it is not good for them. Change, even positive change, can feel unsafe because it is unknown. When life starts to improve, part of us may try to pull it back to what we are used to. Understanding this can turn frustration into curiosity: 'What part of me feels unsafe with this change?'"),
("Meeting the need differently", "Once the need is visible, the question shifts from 'How do I stop this behaviour?' to 'How can I meet this need in a healthier way?' If procrastination protects you from fear of judgement, perhaps you can share early drafts with one trusted person to make judgement feel less threatening. If overspending soothes stress, perhaps there are other ways to soothe yourself. The behaviour often loosens its grip once the underlying need is acknowledged."),
("When to seek support", "Some patterns are deep and connected to painful experiences. If self-sabotage feels overwhelming or tied to difficult memories, speaking with a qualified professional can help. This lesson is for general reflection, not therapy."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the unsent application", "Karan, a junior architect in Chandigarh, had wanted to apply for a design fellowship for two years. Each time the deadline approached, he found reasons to delay until it passed. When he reflected honestly, he realised that applying meant risking rejection by people he admired, and not applying protected him from that hurt. Seeing this did not make the fear disappear, but it helped him plan differently: he asked a mentor to review his portfolio early, which made the process feel less lonely. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Self-sabotage often protects us from something we fear.","Ask what need the behaviour is meeting, not only how to stop it.","Familiar situations can feel safer than better but unknown ones.","Meet the underlying need in a healthier way; seek support if patterns feel heavy."],
reflect=["Think of a time you held yourself back. What might that behaviour have protected you from?","What would it look like to meet that need in a kinder way?"],
)
